WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.934
Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Colin Quinn.

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:05.033
You talk a lot about how
technology is affecting us,

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:07.334
and now technology is creeping
into things like the way we

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:08.534
communicate to each other.
-Sure.

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:12.067
-And the way we write
and make art, ChatGPT.

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:13.234
-Yeah.
A lot of people have been

00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:15.267
talking about this.
It's AI, it can write.

00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:17.534
We -- because you mentioned
technology.

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:21.701
We asked ChatGPT to write a
Colin Quinn stand-up routine.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:23.000
-That's fine, but I don't
know why you had

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.701
to start by blaming me, because
you mentioned technology.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.100
Like, as if I demanded this.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.734
You guys are blaming me
for my own demise.

00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:33.000
Go ahead.
-You haven't seen it.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.000
-Didn't see it.
-Alright.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.300
You have not seen it.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.501
This is very exciting
to see if it confuses you.

00:00:36.501 --> 00:00:38.067
-Oh, I have to do it.
-You have to read it.

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.133
Alright.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:43.234
Um, this is my stand-up act.

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:45.734
"Hey, folks,
welcome to the show.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:48.834
Now, I've gotta say, things
getting pretty crazy out there.

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:50.300
I mean, you turn on the news,
it's like,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.367
'What the hell is happening?'"

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.133
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.868
-How are you feeling
about it so far?

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:57.367
-I'm feeling horrified.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.367
"But, you know what?

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.100
I don't even bother watching
the news anymore.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.367
I just go on social media
and read the comments.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:06.200
That's where you really get
the real news.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.367
You know, the news
that really matters."

00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:10.667
I like to reiterate,
apparently.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.801
-[ Laughs ]
You should feel good.

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:14.267
This is so bad,
you should feel good

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:15.934
about the fact that
you're outwriting

00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:17.634
circles around this computer.
-Well, you know,

00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:19.501
but it's kind of like getting
to an essence of me

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:20.534
that I'm not happy with.

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:22.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.701
"Like, I can't believe this
country is going to hell."

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:25.934
I think I said that.

00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:27.634
"And I'm moving to Canada."

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:29.133
That must be the big punch line.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:32.167
"Why does everyone --
why does everyone hate us?

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.234
Then you get these politicians,
oh, boy, the politicians.

00:01:34.234 --> 00:01:36.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:38.000
"They're like the weather.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.534
Nobody really likes them, but
you've got to deal with them.

00:01:39.534 --> 00:01:41.501
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.033
And you know what they say
about politicians?

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.901
they're like diapers,
they need to be changed often

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:48.000
for the same reason."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.901
-Okay, that's more
than enough of that.

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:51.000
-That was great.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.367
-I think you're good.

00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:55.167
What I'm saying is
I think you're good.

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.167
-That was beautiful and
horrifying at the same time.

00:01:58.167 --> 00:01:59.767
Can I have that for my show?
-Of course.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:02.234
You can have it for later
tonight. Let's see how it goes.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:04.534
But this goes to the questions
of authenticity,

00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:07.200
and I think with AI
and with things like this,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.667
people are worried about
will we be able

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.367
to recognize authenticity.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:13.400
People claim they want
authenticity all the time,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.400
but you don't think
that's the case.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.801
-No, authenticity is the --

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:17.133
that's the one thing we don't.

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:18.400
We're trying to get away
from it.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.801
Animals are authentic.

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:20.901
That's the whole point.

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:22.367
We're trying to get away from --

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:23.467
Like, you don't want --
everybody's like oh,

00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:24.834
these politicians are fake.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:26.901
Yeah, that's what we want --
a fake.

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:28.534
You don't want a real president.

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:29.801
You don't want somebody
coming out,

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:32.834
like a worried president, coming
out and being like,

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:35.868
"For state of the union,
oh God, oh God.

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:38.701
Oh, no. Putin is really
going to do it."

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:41.467
HE starts crying in the middle
of the thing.

00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:42.901
You want a guy that comes out
and goes our

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:44.400
best days are ahead of us.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.467
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:48.100
-What do you think of --
I mean, George Santos,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.934
do you think he's inauthentic
or is he maybe

00:02:49.934 --> 00:02:51.300
the most authentic politician?

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.133
-First of all, yeah.

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:56.167
I mean, he's social media, which
you can be whatever you decide

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:58.667
to be that day, everybody gets
to be whatever we want.

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:01.167
So he's like, hey, today
I'm this, today I'm that.

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:03.167
And -- but it is funny,
isn't it?

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:06.701
That you get -- that's not --
you have to resign.

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:09.033
Lying is not part of your job.

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:10.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:12.033
Because they said things like,
"What if somebody lies?"

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.033
They're like, "Do you want
to really get rid of him?

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:15.534
I mean..."

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:18.667
-I was surprised to hear this,
that you were a huge

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:21.067
James Joyce fan,
and that James Joyce

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.501
actually inspired not, I guess,
the way you wrote.

00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:24.901
But would you think the way
you wrote?

00:03:24.901 --> 00:03:26.467
-Oh, the way I wrote too, yeah.
-Okay.

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.801
-When I started comedy,
I would --

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.701
First of all, before I started
comedy, not that I was

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:34.167
a pretentious guy, I'd walk
around with a copy of "Ulysses"

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:38.501
in my pocket, sit around to hope
people would notice "Ulysses."

00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:40.067
-Pretty big book.
-Pretty big book,

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:44.434
but I always --
I always had a copy, you know?

00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:47.033
But Joyce speaks,
Ulysses especially,

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:48.667
speaks in a run-on sentence.

00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:52.167
So the whole book is like one --
there's no periods.

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:54.968
So when I started comedy, I was
like, I'm going to be like

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:58.834
the Irish, kind of, you know,
mavericks, cerebral.

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:01.033
Nobody was asking for this,
but just wait a second.

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:02.701
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:03.968
There was no reason,
it was the mid '80s.

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:05.601
Now you say I want
to separate myself.

00:04:05.601 --> 00:04:07.234
Back then you
didn't even have to,

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:09.400
but I chose my path.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.334
So I go on stage
and just never even break,

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:15.267
and so there's no place
for people to laugh,

00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:17.400
even if they had
chosen to laugh.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.334
So I just kept talking,
and then eventually

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:21.133
if people don't laugh in
stand-up,

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:25.167
even if they're being amused, it
feels like a bomb to everybody.

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:27.000
I'd be getting mad at them
for not laughing,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.534
even though there's no place
to get a laugh.

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:29.767
They'd be getting mean because
they're not getting

00:04:29.767 --> 00:04:31.234
laughs at a comedy show.

00:04:31.234 --> 00:04:33.067
It was very ugly few years.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:35.133
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:37.467
And to this day,
I still obviously have

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:41.367
an element of it
in my presentation.

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:43.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.567
-I didn't know.

00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:46.167
This is something
you discovered.

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:50.100
James Joyce was not just
a writer, he was a singer.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.868
-&gt;&gt; He was a second best singer
in all of Ireland.

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:55.467
-Who is the -- it's such
a funny title to be

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:57.133
the second best singer
in all of Ireland.

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:58.601
-I mean, it's great.
I guess he he was like --

00:04:58.601 --> 00:05:00.801
he was like "The Banshees
of Inisherin."

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:01.901
-Yeah.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.167
-I wasn't going to say
Martin McDonagh,

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:05.968
director reference either,
by the way.

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:07.934
Because that would have been
like, you know.

00:05:07.934 --> 00:05:10.100
I don't know what.
Um, yeah.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.567
He was a great singer,
and his wife wanted him

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:14.601
to be a singer, instead he
decided to write these books,

00:05:14.601 --> 00:05:17.968
you know, these rambling books
that annoyed people.

00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:20.400
-Is that how -- his rambling
books are annoying you?

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.167
-I mean, "Finnegans Wake,"
have you tried to read it?

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:24.067
I've tried to read it 30 times.

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:26.667
I've read books about
"Finnegans Wake"

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:28.567
to understand it, and those
books don't even understand it.

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:31.567
-How many -- So, okay, I've
tried dense books before,

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:34.467
and I will usually say my bail
point is 25, 30 pages.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:36.400
Is that how far you've gotten
into before you like --

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.467
-No, no, I've read
"Finnegans Wake

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:41.167
and I've Joseph Campbell's book
on "Finnegans Wake,"

00:05:41.167 --> 00:05:42.601
and he really -- you can tell --

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:43.968
Joseph Cambell's like a genius,

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:45.200
he has no idea about
what Finnegans --

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.200
I know more about it
than he did.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.167
I'm reading this going,
"What's he talking about?"

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:50.434
I don't think he read it.

00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:52.701
-Well, I think -- I think
everybody who watches this

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:55.334
is going to run out
and buy a "Finnegans Wake."

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:56.934
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:05:56.934 --> 00:06:01.000
You guys, "Small talk," Lucille
Lortel Theater,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.367
That's Colin Quinn,
we'll be right back

00:06:02.367 --> 00:06:03.601
with Gabrielle union.

